{"corpus_git_commit":"ed6a70d92e27c681d86485e0581020efea381304","evidence":["M-20260914T053745Z-frontend_lexical_v2-65ef003f"],"observed_at":"2026-09-14T05:38:06.494299+00:00","record_id":"O-20260914T053806Z-d6513ce3","record_type":"observation","recorded_at":"2026-09-14T05:38:06.494299+00:00","repository_state":{"commit":"ed6a70d92e27c681d86485e0581020efea381304","state_sha256":"e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855","status":[],"untracked_files":[],"worktree_clean":true},"schema_version":1,"tags":["baseline","reproducibility"],"text":"A clean full-provenance rerun of the deployed lexical-v2 baseline reproduces the earlier ranking metrics exactly: Owner Hit@10=0.583, Hit@10=0.792, Source Hit@10=0.958, MRR=0.548, and nDCG@10=0.413. This confirms that the documented weakness is not an artifact of the legacy report format."}
{"corpus_git_commit":"783f0cc25540ba39f7f99610e70b73858cedc3f6","evidence":["M-20260914T053745Z-frontend_lexical_v2-65ef003f","M-20260914T052204Z-gemini_multiquery_rrf_v1-2bbd7849","M-20260914T052403Z-gemini_multiquery_rrf_cohere_v4_fast_v1-b1369345"],"observed_at":"2026-09-14T05:47:52.176513+00:00","record_id":"H-20260914T054752Z-08aabc89","record_type":"hypothesis","recorded_at":"2026-09-14T05:47:52.176513+00:00","repository_state":{"commit":"783f0cc25540ba39f7f99610e70b73858cedc3f6","state_sha256":"e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855","status":[],"untracked_files":[],"worktree_clean":true},"schema_version":1,"tags":["ranking","reranking","candidate-generation","hybrid-retrieval"],"text":"The most immediately obvious near-term way to improve search is to retrieve a broader candidate set and then rerank it aggressively. The current measurements make this a concrete hypothesis rather than a generic recommendation: deployed lexical v2 has Owner Hit@10 = 0.583 but Source Hit@10 = 0.958; multi-query RRF raises Owner Hit@10 to 0.750; multi-query RRF plus reranking raises Owner Hit@10 to 0.958 and Hit@10 to 1.000. This suggests that the corpus and coarse lexical retrieval often find the right neighborhood, while the raw file ranking frequently buries the actual owner formalization below import aggregators, adjacent modules, support files, or another file in the correct source.\n\nThe immediate architecture to test is therefore: keep the current lexical search as a cheap first stage; retrieve substantially more candidates, probably 50\u2013100 rather than displaying its raw top 10; use modest query expansion/reformulation to improve candidate recall; and rerank those candidates using richer information such as source name, full module/file path, filename, declaration names/signatures where extractable, nearby docstrings/comments, bounded source excerpts, and query terms/synonyms. Display only the reranked top results.\n\nThe existing Cohere experiment already demonstrates that this general shape can work, but the exact prototype should not be promoted directly because its cost and latency are not yet acceptable serving targets. A controlled next experiment should determine how much of the gain can be recovered with cheaper deterministic or learned reranking signals. Particularly obvious deterministic signals include penalizing umbrella/import-only files such as Mathlib.lean; rewarding files whose path/name resembles the mathematical concept; rewarding files containing matching declarations rather than merely imports; and distinguishing theorem/definition-bearing files from roadmap, audit, index, and support files.\n\nAfter that, test an independent semantic first-stage retriever\u2014embeddings, learned sparse retrieval, or another semantic channel\u2014because the lexical candidate-depth audit showed a ceiling: Owner Hit@20, @50, @100, and @150 plateau around 0.625. No reranker can recover an owner file absent from the candidate pool. The measured priority hypothesis is therefore: better candidate generation plus second-stage reranking first, followed immediately by dense/hybrid retrieval; do not wholesale-replace Zoekt, and do not fine-tune a model before these staged retrieval hypotheses have been measured."}
